# Aston Martin 1.5-litre (Bamford & Martin) sports, 1922-1925.
#
# The first Bamford & Martin Aston Martin: the 1.5-litre side-valve four in a
# short-wheelbase sporting chassis, with the boat-tail body and the spare
# wheel recessed into the tail. The pack photographs the Standard Sports
# body; other bodies were built to order.
#
# Dimensions: NO manufacturer figure was found. The model measures 3.68 x
# 1.51 x 1.65 m (hood raised) on a 2.49 m wheelbase, ESTIMATED from the
# photographs and assumed tyre outside diameters; these are not
# specifications and must not be quoted as such.
#
# Shape reference: a cream and brown boat-tail car, plate PD 8111, in true
# level side elevation, front three-quarter and rear three-quarter.
#
# Colours: grade (d). No British colour list exists; cars were finished to the
# buyer's order, usually one colour with contrasting wings. The objects carry
# plain names (green, black, red, silver); the photographed cream and brown
# scheme is one customer's choice, not a catalogue colour.
#
# Speed 130 km/h, an estimate for the 1.5-litre sports. Comfort 25/368 on the
# line 330 + 1.5 x 25: an open, short, hard-riding sports car. Classes 2,3,4.
# Dates are road presence: 1922 intro, retires staggered 1940-1943.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/aston-1half-litre.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/aston-1half-litre.dae. References:
# citycars/references/aston-1half-litre/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons).
#
# Accepted deviations: the hood sides are too closed and the front wing sweep
# is understated. At 128 px the boat-tail, exposed spare wheel and raised hood
# read as a 1920s Aston Martin.


obj=citycar
name=Aston1HalfLitre(Green)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=2
intro_year=1922
intro_month=1
retire_year=1943
retire_month=6
speed=130
length=2
class=2,3,4
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=25
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=368

